$DIR/main.p:32:9: cannot pass st to st.String (which may call main.(*S).String), which keeps it after returning
    st is an interface that points at a copy of its value in show's frame.
    Converting a value larger than one word to an interface puts a copy of the
    value in the frame of the function that converts it (here show, at
    main.p:31), and the interface points at that copy. st.String (which may call
    main.(*S).String) stores s in package variable keep (main.p:12), so the
    interface would outlive show's frame.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Change st.String (which may call main.(*S).String) to take a value or an
    owner, which it can keep: an owned[T] from new, a shared[T], or a library
    owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)
